BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pedantic-arts-residency-and-research-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pedantic Arts Residency and Research Grant 2027 (Pittsbur
 gh)
DESCRIPTION:Pedantic Arts Residency opens applications for its Summer 2027 
 and Fall 2027 programmes in Pittsburgh: two four-week residency cohorts an
 d two research grants. THE RESIDENCY houses three creatives at a time (a v
 isual artist\, an organizer and a writer) in a collective four-week progra
 mme that emphasizes exploration\, exchange and community connection over p
 roduction. Residents get free furnished housing in a shared apartment\, a 
 USD 2\,000 stipend to cover living expenses (half on arrival\, half on com
 pletion)\, a Pittsburgh-based guide working in their field\, tickets and i
 nstitutional passes\, and introductions across the city's creative communi
 ty. THE RESEARCH GRANT invites an individual visual artist\, curator or wr
 iter for a one- to two-week independent stay to conduct Pittsburgh-related
  research\, with a free private bedroom at the Pedantic Apartment\, a USD 
 500 or USD 1\,000 honorarium usable at the grantee's discretion\, and help
  establishing relevant connections. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be 18 or 
 older and not Pittsburgh residents at the time of application\; travel cos
 ts are the participant's own. Residency attendance and staying in the prov
 ided housing are required. APPLICATION: applications are free until 28 Sep
 tember 2026 at 23:59 EST\; a USD 20 fee applies from then until the final 
 deadline of 15 October 2026 at 23:59 EST.\n\nOrganisation: Pedantic Arts R
 esidency\nLocation: Pittsburgh\, Pennsylvania\, USA. Open to non-Pittsburg
 h residents aged 18 or older\; travel is self-funded.\nAward: Residency: f
 ree furnished housing in a shared apartment plus a USD 2\,000 living stipe
 nd (half on arrival\, half on completion). Research Grant: a free private 
 bedroom at the Pedantic Apartment plus a USD 500 or USD 1\,000 honorarium 
 at the grantee's discretion. Application free until 28 September 2026\, th
 en USD 20.\nDuration: Residency: four weeks\, Summer 2027 or Fall 2027 coh
 orts. Research Grant: one to two weeks.\n\nMore: https://pedanticarts.org/
 announcements/open-call-for-residents-and-grantees-submit-by-october-15th/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pedanticarts.org/announcements/open-call-for-residents-and-gran
 tees-submit-by-october-15th/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Pittsburgh\, Pennsylvania\, USA. Open to non-Pittsburgh residents 
 aged 18 or older\; travel is self-funded.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surels-place-residency-2027-winter@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surel's Place: Month-Long Artist Residency (September 202
 6 deadline)
DESCRIPTION:Surel's Place is a residency in Garden City\, Idaho with a two-
 fold mission: to give artists the time\, shelter and professional support 
 their work deserves\, and to give the public accessible art experiences in
 cluding performances\, readings\, exhibits and workshops. In exchange for 
 the residency\, artists offer art experiences back to the community. OPEN 
 TO professional visual\, literary and performance artists: painters\, writ
 ers\, musicians\, architects\, filmmakers\, choreographers\, any artist wh
 o needs a place to focus. LIMITS: because of the property's constraints an
 d proximity to neighbours\, it cannot accommodate loud or very messy proce
 sses such as welding or blacksmithing. The organisers also note that predo
 minantly conceptual work\, work that must be explained to attract a viewer
 \, may not be met with as much enthusiasm by the jurors\, and that they we
 lcome artists who want to connect with an audience. Each cycle\, one of th
 e five residencies includes outreach to local schools through the PARTNER 
 Schools Program. REQUIREMENTS: maintain residence at the home and studio (
 no studio-use-only residencies)\; use the residency in a focused way towar
 ds a stated goal with no other employment or work happening simultaneously
 \; visual artists give a show of and/or lecture about their residency work
 \, writers give a reading and discussion session\; all artists offer at le
 ast one public workshop (2 to 3 hours\, minimal lecturing)\; visual artist
 s donate one piece of art to Surel's Place for future fundraising\; writer
 s allow Surel's Place to publish a free-standing excerpt of their residenc
 y work in books\, broadsides\, posters or similar. SALES COMMISSION applie
 s as set out in the funding notes. HOUSE RULES: the non-smoking requiremen
 t is non-negotiable and any evidence of smoking is grounds for early termi
 nation. Partners are welcome but children and pets cannot be accommodated\
 , no more than two adults may live there during a residency\, and partners
  wanting concurrent residencies must apply and be accepted separately. ELI
 GIBILITY: applicants must be 25 or older and non-smokers. APPLY by email t
 o info@surelsplace.org with the online Artist in Residence application plu
 s\, for VISUAL ARTISTS\, a cover letter introducing yourself and saying wh
 y you want to be at Surel's Place specifically\, a proposal including a wo
 rkshop outline\, a CV\, electronic images of 4 to 6 pieces of work and an 
 image list (title\, size\, medium\, year)\; for LITERARY applicants\, cove
 r letter\, proposal with workshop outline\, CV and PDF work samples with y
 our name and page number in the top right corner (fiction\, prose or scrip
 t no more than 10 typed double-spaced pages\; poetry no more than six poem
 s and no more than 10 pages)\; for OTHER MEDIUMS\, cover letter\, proposal
  with workshop outline\, CV and electronic samples (contact Surel's Place 
 to discuss sample requirements for your medium). Applications are reviewed
  by juries of accomplished artists\, writers and fine arts professionals\,
  and all applicants are notified by email.\n\nOrganisation: Surel's Place\
 nLocation: In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents 
 must live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.\nAward: Fre
 e rent\, utilities and wi-fi\, a living stipend of USD 100 per week\, a US
 D 300 travel stipend\, use of well-maintained bicycles\, free or discounte
 d tickets to art events and movies\, marketing through Surel's Place chann
 els\, and event hosting including logistics and installation help. Each re
 sident also gets a local host to orient them and host their public events.
  No application fee. NOTE: Surel's Place takes a commission on sales arisi
 ng from the residency\, 35% of retail price on artwork\, 45% on ticketed e
 vents that are offsite or need contracted services (calculated after admin
 istrative expenses)\, and 15% on books\, CDs or other merchandise tangenti
 al to the artist's medium. A surcharge may apply to cover additional utili
 ty costs if a partner joins.\nDuration: One month (longer may be possible 
 if justified in the application). The 1 September deadline covers residenc
 ies in January to May of the following year\, beginning four months after 
 the deadline\; the 1 March deadline covers July to November of the same ye
 ar.\n\nMore: https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents mus
 t live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-arts-fellowship-2027-2029@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Princeton Arts Fellowship 2027-2029 (Lewis Center for the
  Arts)
DESCRIPTION:The Princeton Arts Fellowship places early-career artists at th
 e Lewis Center for the Arts for two academic years on a full-time salaried
  appointment\, on the conviction that exposure to the arts helps us make s
 ense of our lives and the lives of our neighbours. ELIGIBLE APPLICANTS are
  early-career composers\, conductors\, musicians\, choreographers\, playwr
 ights\, designers\, directors and performance artists. IMPORTANT FOR THIS 
 CYCLE: applications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Progr
 am in Theater and Music Theater\, and the Department of Music. Fellows typ
 ically teach one undergraduate course per semester (subject to approval by
  the Office of the Dean of the Faculty)\, or in some cases take on an arti
 stic assignment with students in lieu of a class\; prior teaching experien
 ce is NOT required. Beyond that\, fellows are expected to engage with the 
 Princeton community through advising students\, talks\, workshops\, exhibi
 tions or other events\, and have time to pursue their own artistic practic
 e and scholarly research. NOT ELIGIBLE: the appointment cannot be used to 
 fund work leading to a PhD or any advanced degree\; holders of Princeton P
 hDs cannot apply\; past recipients of the Hodder Fellowship cannot apply\;
  and anyone with a sustained and continuous relationship with Princeton is
  excluded (an occasional and sporadic relationship is fine). Fellows may n
 ot hold concurrent teaching positions at other institutions during the app
 ointment. You may apply once per application cycle. APPLICATION via Interf
 olio: a CV\; a 750-word proposal broken into how you would use the fellows
 hip to develop your work (250 words)\, what you might teach or pursue with
  undergraduates (250 words)\, and your approach to building community with
  people of different backgrounds\, experiences\, perspectives and artistic
  practices through your practice\, teaching and/or research (250 words)\; 
 contact information for one reference\; and work samples with a 150-word s
 tatement on how they relate to your proposal\, submitted as links in a PDF
 . WORK SAMPLE SPECS: Dance (choreography)\, one to three links to performa
 nces\, rehearsals or research\, continuous footage only\, no trailers\, ph
 otographs or promotional material\, with cue points of no more than 3 to 4
  minutes per link and no more than 10 minutes total. Music\, one to two sc
 ores and/or links to performance examples totalling 10 minutes\, video opt
 ional. Theater\, up to 15 pages of writing sample and/or up to 10 design i
 mages (photos or renderings) and/or a video or audio reel with cue points\
 , no more than 10 minutes to watch. The deadline is 8 September 2026 at 11
 :59 pm EDT\; receipt of applications cannot be confirmed and late applicat
 ions are not accepted. FAQ and examples of building community in artistic 
 practice\, teaching and research: arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/faq.\n\nO
 rganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\nLocation: I
 n person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees are expecte
 d to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, engaged i
 nteraction with students. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship
  of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.\nAward: USD 93\,000 salary
  per year for each of two academic years\, as a full-time 24-month appoint
 ment at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Two academic years\, 2027-2028 and 2028-2029. Applications ope
 n 1 July 2026 and close 8 September 2026\; finalists attend an on-campus i
 nterview during the week of 7 December 2026.\n\nMore: https://apply.interf
 olio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply.interfolio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees ar
 e expected to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, 
 engaged interaction with students. These positions are not eligible for sp
 onsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cullman-center-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cullman Center Fellowships 2027-28 (New York Public Libra
 ry)
DESCRIPTION:The Cullman Center for Scholars and Writers at the New York Pub
 lic Library awards fifteen fellowships a year to writers and scholars (fic
 tion\, nonfiction\, poetry\, playwriting\, translation\, and academic rese
 arch) whose projects draw on the Library's collections. Fellows receive US
 D 90\,000\, an office\, and full research access for a September-May term 
 in residence. Open internationally. Deadline 25 September 2026\, 5pm EDT\;
  apply via the NYPL online application portal.\n\nOrganisation: Dorothy an
 d Lewis B. Cullman Center for Scholars and Writers\, New York Public Libra
 ry\nLocation: New York City\, United States. Open internationally\; fellow
 s work in residence at NYPL's Stephen A. Schwarzman Building.\nAward: USD 
 90\,000 stipend\, plus an office at the library\, full access to collectio
 ns\, and a September-May term. No application fee.\nDuration: September 20
 27 to May 2028\, in residence at the New York Public Library.\n\nMore: htt
 ps://www.nypl.org/about/fellowships-institutes/cullman-center-scholars-wri
 ters/fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.nypl.org/about/fellowships-institutes/cullman-center-schola
 rs-writers/fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:New York City\, United States. Open internationally\; fellows work
  in residence at NYPL's Stephen A. Schwarzman Building.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:woodward-newman-award-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Woodward/Newman Award 2027-28 (Constellation Stage & Scre
 en)
DESCRIPTION:The Woodward/Newman Award\, started with the support of Joanne 
 Woodward\, Newman's Own Foundation and the Newman family\, is awarded annu
 ally by Constellation Stage & Screen to the best unpublished new play. The
  winner receives a USD 3\,000 cash prize (in lieu of royalties) and a full
  production in the Mainstage season\, with housing and transportation prov
 ided. The contest format and submission fees have been eliminated: submiss
 ions are reviewed on an ongoing basis and are also considered for all seve
 n production slots in the season\, including TYA (theatre for young audien
 ces) shows. Full-length plays should run 75 to 135 minutes\; TYA shows ove
 r 40 minutes. Plays must be unpublished at submission (independently publi
 shed is acceptable)\; limit of two submissions per playwright per year\; m
 usicals may include demos. Plays submitted by midnight 31 August 2026 are 
 considered for the 2027-28 season\; later submissions roll to 2028-29. All
  submissions are kept on file for two years.\n\nOrganisation: Constellatio
 n Stage & Screen\nLocation: Bloomington\, Indiana\, USA. Open submissions 
 of unpublished plays\; production takes place in Bloomington.\nAward: USD 
 3\,000 cash prize (in lieu of royalties) plus a full production in Constel
 lation's Mainstage season\; housing and transportation provided. No submis
 sion fee.\nDuration: Winner announced by 15 May 2027\; production in the 2
 027-28 season. Submissions stay on file and under consideration for two ye
 ars.\n\nMore: https://seeconstellation.org/get-involved/submit-play/?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://seeconstellation.org/get-involved/submit-play/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Bloomington\, Indiana\, USA. Open submissions of unpublished plays
 \; production takes place in Bloomington.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:leon-levy-biography-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270104
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270105
SUMMARY:Deadline: Leon Levy Center for Biography Fellowships 2027-28 (incl.
  Levy/Sloan Science Biography)
DESCRIPTION:Four resident biography fellowships plus the Levy/Sloan Fellows
 hip\, funded by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation\, for a biography of a figu
 re in science or technology\; separate Sherwin fellowship for nuclear-age 
 history. Open to writers of all nationalities\, with explicit preference f
 or first-time biographers who have not yet held a biography fellowship\, t
 hough mid-career and senior writers are welcome\; independent writers elig
 ible. Fellows join monthly seminars and the center's public events. Apply 
 with CV\, career narrative\, project description\, writing sample and two 
 references (agents\, editors and publishers are discouraged as referees). 
 No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Leon Levy Center for Biography\, CUNY
  Graduate Center\nLocation: New York City\, USA (in residence at the CUNY 
 Graduate Center\, September to May)\nAward: $72\,000 stipend plus writing 
 space\, full research facilities and research assistance\nDuration: Septem
 ber 2027 to May 2028 (academic year in residence)\n\nMore: https://theleon
 levycenterforbiography.submittable.com/submit?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://theleonlevycenterforbiography.submittable.com/submit?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City\, USA (in residence at the CUNY Graduate Center\, Se
 ptember to May)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:harry-ransom-center-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261106
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261107
SUMMARY:Deadline: Harry Ransom Center Research Fellowships 2027-2028 (Austi
 n\, Texas)
DESCRIPTION:The Harry Ransom Center at The University of Texas at Austin aw
 ards research fellowships for on-site work in its collections spanning lit
 erature\, photography\, film\, art\, performing arts\, music and cultural 
 history\, supporting scholarly articles\, dissertations\, creative works\,
  digital humanities projects and exhibition proposals. FELLOWSHIP TYPES: P
 ostdoctoral Fellowships of USD 3\,500 per month for one or two months\, op
 en to holders of a PhD or terminal degree (MFA\, MLIS)\, current or former
  faculty\, and similarly qualified researchers without institutional affil
 iation\; Dissertation Fellowships of USD 2\,500 for graduate students with
  doctoral dissertations in progress\; and Travel Stipends of USD 2\,000 op
 en to graduate students\, current or former faculty and independent resear
 chers including journalists and artists (not combinable with other Ransom 
 Center fellowships). International recipients in all categories receive an
  additional one-time USD 500 stipend toward visa and travel costs. Fellows
  spend most days in the Reading Room consulting the collections\, with acc
 ess to a shared office\, during a residency period between 1 June 2027 and
  31 August 2028. APPLY with a three-page proposal PDF and letters of recom
 mendation\; recommendation letters are also due by the deadline. DEADLINE:
  6 November 2026\, 5:00 PM CST.\n\nOrganisation: Harry Ransom Center\, The
  University of Texas at Austin\nLocation: Austin\, Texas\, USA. On-site re
 search in the Ransom Center's collections\; international applicants welco
 me and receive an additional one-time USD 500 stipend for visa and travel 
 costs.\nAward: Postdoctoral Fellowships: USD 3\,500 per month for one or t
 wo months. Dissertation Fellowships: USD 2\,500. Travel Stipends: USD 2\,0
 00 (open to graduate students\, faculty and independent researchers includ
 ing artists and journalists). International recipients get an extra USD 50
 0. No application fee.\nDuration: One to two months on-site\, taken betwee
 n 1 June 2027 and 31 August 2028.\n\nMore: https://www.hrc.utexas.edu/fell
 owships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.hrc.utexas.edu/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Austin\, Texas\, USA. On-site research in the Ransom Center's coll
 ections\; international applicants welcome and receive an additional one-t
 ime USD 500 stipend for visa and travel costs.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nieman-fellowship-class-of-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Nieman Fellowships at Harvard\, Class of 2028
DESCRIPTION:The Nieman Foundation brings around two dozen journalists to Ha
 rvard each year for two semesters of study\, reflection and community\, on
 e of the oldest and best-known midcareer journalism fellowships in the wor
 ld. Fellows design their own course of study\, audit classes across Harvar
 d's schools and take part in Nieman's own seminars and master classes\; ma
 ny use the year to deepen a beat such as technology\, AI accountability\, 
 science or press freedom. WHAT YOU GET: a USD 87\,500 stipend paid over th
 e nine-month academic year (2026-27 figure)\, plus allowances toward housi
 ng\, childcare and health insurance. WHO IS ELIGIBLE: working journalists\
 , staff or freelance\, in any medium\, with at least five years of full-ti
 me media experience\; open to applicants from any country. Freelancers may
  use a recent supervising editor for one of the three required recommendat
 ion letters. Special fellowships within the program include the Nieman-Ber
 kman Klein Fellowship in Journalism Innovation. TO APPLY: applications for
  the class of 2028 open in the first week of October 2026 and require two 
 essays (personal statement and study proposal)\, work samples and three re
 commendation letters. DEADLINE: 1 December 2026 for international applican
 ts\; 31 January 2027 for US citizens.\n\nOrganisation: Nieman Foundation f
 or Journalism at Harvard\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA (resid
 ential academic year at Harvard)\nAward: USD 87\,500 stipend paid over the
  nine-month academic year (2026-27 figure)\, plus additional allowances fo
 r housing\, childcare and health insurance. Fellows audit courses across H
 arvard and participate in Nieman seminars\, master classes and workshops. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Two semesters in residence at Harvard\, aca
 demic year 2027-28\n\nMore: https://nieman.harvard.edu/fellowships/how-to-
 apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nieman.harvard.edu/fellowships/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA (residential academic year at Harv
 ard)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stigler-center-journalists-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261030T153614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260920
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260921
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stigler Center: Journalists in Residence Program Spring 2
 027 (Chicago Booth)
DESCRIPTION:The Stigler Center at the University of Chicago Booth School of
  Business invites journalists from around the world to its Journalists in 
 Residence Program\, a roughly 12-week residency on the Hyde Park campus in
  spring 2027 focused on the interactions between politics\, business\, mar
 kets and the economy. Running since 2017\, the program has hosted journali
 sts from outlets including the BBC\, The New York Times and ABC News. WHAT
  YOU DO: audit three Chicago Booth classes\, participate actively in class
  discussions and complete required readings and assignments\, attend Stigl
 er Center seminars\, lectures\, conferences and other activities\, collabo
 rate with peers\, network with faculty and researchers\, and explore oppor
 tunities to write for the Center's ProMarket publication. WHAT YOU GET: US
 D 14\,000 stipend for living expenses\, economy-class airfare reimbursemen
 t\, SEVIS and visa application fee coverage\, health insurance through UCh
 icago\, housing guidance\, academic opportunities and a certificate upon c
 ompletion. Note that the stipend is subject to up to 14 percent tax withho
 lding for international participants and arrives after roughly the first m
 onth\, so you need funds to bridge that period. ELIGIBILITY: open to journ
 alists from all countries and media backgrounds\, including freelancers an
 d editors\, with some years of professional experience\, proficiency in En
 glish and a genuine interest in political economy and business reporting. 
 Successful applicants are notified by the end of December 2026. DEADLINE: 
 20 September 2026\, 11:59 pm Chicago time (CT).\n\nOrganisation: Stigler C
 enter\, University of Chicago Booth School of Business\nLocation: Universi
 ty of Chicago Booth School of Business\, Hyde Park campus\, Chicago\, USA.
  Open to journalists from all countries and media backgrounds.\nAward: USD
  14\,000 stipend to cover living expenses over the 12-week program\, plus 
 reimbursement of economy-class airfare to and from Chicago\, SEVIS and vis
 a application fees\, health insurance through UChicago\, and housing guida
 nce. The stipend is subject to up to 14 percent tax withholding for intern
